{'id': '2607.12786', 'categories': ['cs.CV'], 'pdf': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.12786', 'abs': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.12786', 'authors': ['Lin Peng', 'Cong Wan', 'Zeyu Guo', 'SongLin Dong', 'Yihong Gong'], 'title': 'CoRe: A Comprehensive Framework for Cross-Image Comparative Reasoning in Vision-Language Models', 'comment': 'Accepted by ACMMM2026', 'summary': 'Cross-image comparative reasoning remains challenging for vision-language models (VLMs), especially when correct prediction requires fine-grained attribute grounding and globally consistent reasoning. We present CoRe, a unified framework for this problem. CoRe includes: (i) CoRe-20K, a large-scale triplet-based training set automatically constructed from structured visual metadata through a multi-expert collaborative pipeline, covering counting, depth, distance, and spatial relations; (ii) TriSR, a structured reward framework that jointly supervises attribute grounding, judgment alignment, and triplet consistency under GRPO optimization; and (iii) CoRe-Bench, the first benchmark dedicated to fine-grained cross-image comparative reasoning. Experiments show that CoRe substantially outperforms existing VLMs on CoRe-Bench while remaining competitive on standard multimodal benchmarks, achieving a 28.2-point gain in partial accuracy over the strongest baseline.'}
